  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "Fixed: a ¥1,500 invoice now produces a gateway amount of 1500, not 150000. `npm test` passes with 8 of 8 tests.\n\n**Root cause:** `buildChargePayload` in `src/payments/gateway/payload.js` lower-cases the currency (`jpy`) for the gateway. It then passed that lower-case code to `toMinor`. The currency registry only has upper-case keys, so `jpy` wasn't found and fell back to the default of 2 decimals, which gave 100×. USD and EUR use 2 decimals anyway, so they were unaffected.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **Registry:** `src/lib/currency/registry.js` now upper-cases and trims the code in `exponent`, `isSupported` and `symbol`. Any other caller that passes a lower-case code is covered too.\n- **Payload:** `payload.js` now passes `invoice.currency` (the stored ISO code) to `toMinor`. The lower-case code is only used for the gateway's `currency` field.\n\n**Regression tests** in `test/charge.test.js`:\n- **Charge payloads:** JPY 1500 gives amount 1500 and KRW 20000 gives 20000, with the currency still sent as `jpy`.\n- **Lookup:** `toMinor(1500, 'jpy')` returns 1500 and `toMinor(1.5, 'kwd')` returns 1500, which checks the lower-case lookup directly.\n\nThe ERP, CRM and tax-provider clients also send lower-case currency codes. I didn't check whether they compute amounts from them. They don't call `toMinor`, so they weren't affected.",
